\section*{Claim}
The singularity classification of \(\widetilde A(\omega)\) directly produces the paper's decay taxonomy.  Simple poles give persistent or exponential oscillatory terms, algebraic branch points give power-law tails, bound-plus-free thresholds give mixed algebraic-exponential tails, and the critical logarithmic edge \(A_{\pm2}(t)\sim e^{\mp2it}/(t\log^2t)\) is asymptotically slower than a generic \(t^{-3/2}\) edge.

\section*{Premises}
\begin{align*}
\text{(P1)}\quad & A(t)=-\frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_{\mathcal B}\widetilde A(\omega)e^{-i\omega t}\,d\omega,\\
\text{(P2)}\quad & \widetilde A(\omega)=\frac{R}{\omega-\omega_p}+O(1)\text{ near a simple pole }\omega_p,\\
\text{(P3)}\quad & \widetilde A(\omega)=K(\omega-\omega_c)^\beta+O((\omega-\omega_c)^{\beta+1})
\text{ across a branch cut, with }\beta>-1,\\
\text{(P4)}\quad & \int_0^\infty x^\beta e^{-ixt}\,dx=\Gamma(\beta+1)e^{-i\pi(\beta+1)/2}t^{-\beta-1}\quad(t>0),\\
\text{(P5)}\quad & \omega_p\in\mathbb R\text{ gives a bound-state oscillation, while }\operatorname{Im}\omega_p<0\text{ gives a decaying exponential},\\
\text{(P6)}\quad & A_{\pm2}(t)\sim c_\pm e^{\mp2it}(t\log^2t)^{-1},\qquad c_\pm\ne0,\\
\text{(P7)}\quad & \lim_{t\to\infty}t^a/(\log t)^b=+\infty\text{ for }a>0,b>0.
\end{align*}

\section*{Derivation}
For a simple pole, deforming the inverse contour across \(\omega_p\) gives the residue contribution
\[
A_p(t)=-R e^{-i\omega_p t}
\]
up to the orientation convention in (P1).  If \(\omega_p\in\mathbb R\), this term has constant magnitude and is a bound-state contribution.  If \(\operatorname{Im}\omega_p<0\), then
\[
|e^{-i\omega_p t}|=e^{\operatorname{Im}\omega_p t},
\]
which decays exponentially.

For a branch point, write \(\omega=\omega_c+x\) along the local cut.  The leading discontinuity has the form \(Kx^\beta\), so (P1), (P3), and (P4) give
\[
A_c(t)\sim
\widetilde K e^{-i\omega_c t}t^{-\beta-1}
\]
for a constant \(\widetilde K\).  If \(\omega_c\in\mathbb R\), this is an algebraic oscillatory tail.  If \(\omega_c=\epsilon_{\sigma i}\pm1\) arises from a bound-plus-free threshold and the one-excitation bound component carries a nonzero imaginary continuation contribution, the same calculation gives a product of an exponential oscillation with an algebraic prefactor; this is the mixed algebraic-exponential case.

Finally, in the critical logarithmic case (P6),
\begin{align*}
|A_{\pm2}(t)|
&\sim |c_\pm|\frac{1}{t\log^2t},\\
\frac{|A_{\pm2}(t)|}{t^{-3/2}}
&\sim |c_\pm|\frac{t^{1/2}}{\log^2t}
\longrightarrow+\infty
\end{align*}
by (P7).  Therefore the logarithmic edge is slower than a generic \(t^{-3/2}\) edge and must be kept as a distinct decay component.

\section*{Final Result}
\[
\boxed{
\begin{gathered}
\text{real pole}\Rightarrow\text{bound-state oscillation},\qquad
\text{non-real continued pole}\Rightarrow\text{collective exponential decay},\\
\text{branch point}\Rightarrow e^{-i\omega_c t}t^{-\beta-1}\text{ algebraic or mixed decay},\qquad
A_{\pm2}(t)\sim \frac{e^{\mp2it}}{t\log^2t}\text{ is slower than }t^{-3/2}.
\end{gathered}}
\]
\hfill$\blacksquare$
